<section class="wyprowadzenie">
<h3 class="tytul-wyprowadzenia">Where the formula comes from</h3>
<p>Take radius <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi>r</mi></math> and coordinates in which the point of tangency <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi>A</mi></math> sits at the origin, the centre at <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mo>(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>)</mo></math> and <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi>B</mi></math> at <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mo>(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>r</mi><mo>)</mo></math>. The tangent from step 2 is then the horizontal axis.</p>
<ol>
<li>The <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mn>30</mn><mo>°</mo></math> ray at the centre gives a right triangle with vertical leg <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi>r</mi></math>, so <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi>A</mi><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>·</mo><mi>tan</mi><mn>30</mn><mo>°</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>r</mi><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt></mfrac></math> and <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>(</mo><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mi>r</mi><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>)</mo></math>.</li>
<li><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi>D</mi></math> is three radii to the right of <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi>C</mi></math>: <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>(</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mi>r</mi><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>)</mo></math>.</li>
<li><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi>B</mi><mi>D</mi></math> spans <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mn>3</mn><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mi>r</mi><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt></mfrac></math> horizontally and <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mn>2</mn><mi>r</mi></math> vertically, so by Pythagoras <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><msup><mrow><mi>B</mi><mi>D</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>(</mo><mn>9</mn><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>6</mn><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>)</mo></math>.</li>
<li>Since <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mfrac><mn>6</mn><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt></math> and <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mn>9</mn><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>40</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></math>, we get <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><msup><mrow><mi>B</mi><mi>D</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mn>40</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt><mo>)</mo></math>.</li>
<li>Hence <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi>B</mi><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>r</mi><msqrt><mfrac><mn>40</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt></msqrt><mo>≈</mo><mn>3.141533</mn><mo>·</mo><mi>r</mi></math>, short of <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi>π</mi><mi>r</mi></math> by about <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mn>6</mn><mo>·</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup><mo>·</mo><mi>r</mi></math>.</li>
</ol>
<p class="dim">The derivation is ours; Kochański gave the construction and the result, not this calculation.</p>
</section>

<p class="note">On a clock dial the error falls below what a seventeenth-century workshop could machine. At the radius of the Earth it grows to nearly four hundred metres.</p>
<p class="note uwaga">The claim that this is an approximation designed for making rather than for calculating is our interpretation — Kochański nowhere argues it in those terms.</p>
